  "question": {
    "text": "Let $C$ be the capacitance of a capacitor discharging through a resistor R. Suppose $t_1$ is the time taken for the energy stored in the capacitor to reduce to half its initial value and $t_2$ is the time taken for the charge to reduce to one-fourth its initial value. Then the ratio $t_1 / t_2$ will be"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n1\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{2}$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$\\frac{1}{4}$\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\n2",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\mathrm{U}=\\frac{1}{2} \\frac{\\mathrm{q}^2}{\\mathrm{C}}=\\frac{1}{2 \\mathrm{C}}\\left(\\mathrm{q}_0 \\mathrm{e}^{-\\mathrm{t} / \\mathrm{T}}\\right)^2=\\frac{\\mathrm{q}_0^2}{2 \\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{e}^{-2 \\mathrm{t} / \\mathrm{T}} \\quad($ where $\\tau=\\mathrm{CR})$\n$\\mathrm{U}=\\mathrm{U}_{\\mathrm{i}} \\mathrm{e}^{-2 \\mathrm{t} / \\mathrm{\\tau}}$\n$\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{U}_{\\mathrm{i}}=\\mathrm{U}_{\\mathrm{i}} \\mathrm{e}^{-2 \\mathrm{t}_1 / \\mathrm{\\tau}}$\n$\\frac{1}{2}=\\mathrm{e}^{-2 \\mathrm{t}_1 / \\tau} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{t}_1=\\frac{\\mathrm{T}}{2} \\ln 2$\nNow $\\quad \\mathrm{q}=\\mathrm{q}_0 \\mathrm{e}^{-\\mathrm{t} / \\mathrm{T}}$\n$\\frac{1}{4} \\mathrm{q}_0=\\mathrm{q}_0 \\mathrm{e}^{-\\mathrm{t} / 2 \\mathrm{~T}}$\n$\\mathrm{t}_2=\\operatorname{T} \\ln 4=2 \\operatorname{T} \\ln 2$\n$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{\\mathrm{t}_1}{\\mathrm{t}_2}=\\frac{1}{4}$"
  }
